From: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: pci: support WLAN MACs integrated in a SoC

The 802.11n WLAN MAC of the Realtek RTL8197F SoC has the host interface
of the PCIe chips (buffer descriptor rings, the same DMA and interrupt
registers), but sits on the SoC bus as a platform device. Let the PCI
bus code drive such a device:

- a probe, remove and shutdown for platform devices, called by the glue
  driver of the SoC once the block is powered up; the DMA API works on
  rtwdev->dev and the IRQ number is kept, so the ring, interrupt and
  NAPI code is shared;
- the queue stop bits of the host interface keep their value across the
  MAC power sequences of the SoC block, and nothing clears them: do so
  in setup, and set them on stop to let the bus settle before the
  buffers go, as the vendor driver does. Run without DMA prefetch and
  multiple tags, and without the PERST# select bit;
- the index of the RX ring can move ahead of the tag the DMA writes;
  poll the tag for up to 1 ms instead of warning right away;
- smaller RX buffers (a page instead of 11 KB, with HT A-MSDUs of up to
  3839 bytes) and 256 instead of 512 of them, which matters on a router
  with 128 MB;
- the efuse of the WLAN MAC is not behind REG_EFUSE_CTRL but in the SoC
  efuse, mapped by the device tree: read it through a new hci op.

Allocate only the RX ring of the MPDU queue for all chips: the C2H ring
was never handed to the hardware.

Signed-off-by: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
---
diff --git drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/efuse.c drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/efuse.c
index b85075c..ab6a258 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/efuse.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/efuse.c
@@ -160,6 +160,16 @@ int rtw_parse_efuse_map(struct rtw_dev *rtwdev)
 		goto out_free;
 	}
 
+	if (rtwdev->hci.ops->read_efuse_map) {
+		ret = rtwdev->hci.ops->read_efuse_map(rtwdev, log_map, log_size);
+		if (ret) {
+			rtw_err(rtwdev, "failed to read efuse map\n");
+			goto out_free;
+		}
+
+		goto parse;
+	}
+
 	ret = rtw_dump_physical_efuse_map(rtwdev, phy_map);
 	if (ret) {
 		rtw_err(rtwdev, "failed to dump efuse physical map\n");
@@ -173,6 +183,8 @@ int rtw_parse_efuse_map(struct rtw_dev *rtwdev)
 		goto out_free;
 	}
 
+parse:
+
 	ret = chip->ops->read_efuse(rtwdev, log_map);
 	if (ret) {
 		rtw_err(rtwdev, "failed to read efuse map\n");
